-- title:
--   Theorem 1.1 — online threshold $\tfrac12L$: Greedy reaches $(1/2-\varepsilon)L$, no online algorithm exceeds $(1/2+\varepsilon)L$
-- statement:
--   For every $\kappa\in(0,1)$ and every $\varepsilon>0$ there are $C=C(\varepsilon,\kappa)>0$ and $N$ such that for all $n\ge N$ and all $C/n\le p\le1-\kappa$, with $L=\log_b(np)$, $b=1/(1-p)$, and $G\sim G(n,p)$:
--
--   1. if $\varepsilon<1/2$, then with probability at least $1-\exp(-n^{\varepsilon})$, for every vertex order and every choice of the $u_t$, the output $M_n$ of Greedy satisfies
--   $$|M_n|\ge\Big(\tfrac12-\varepsilon\Big)L;$$
--   2. every online algorithm as in Definition 2.2, with arbitrary auxiliary randomness independent of $G$, satisfies
--   $$\mathbb P\Big[|M_{\mathrm{out}}|\le\Big(\tfrac12+\varepsilon\Big)L\Big]\ge1-\exp\Big(-\frac{\varepsilon^2}{2}L\log(np)\Big).$$
--
--   This is Theorem 1.1 of the paper in the precise form of its formal versions, Theorems 2.1 and 2.3: a simple online greedy algorithm finds an induced matching of size at least $(1/2-\varepsilon)L$ whp, and every online algorithm finds one of size above $(1/2+\varepsilon)L$ with probability $o(1)$. So half of the maximum, $L$, is the online threshold.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: vertex set [n] = {1, …, n} → Fin n (0-based); edges are Sym2 (Fin n) in G.edgeSet, matchings Finset (Sym2 (Fin n)); reason: relabelling of the vertices; same instances
--   - encoding: G ∼ G(n, p), each pair an edge independently with probability p → the published law LPCommunity.SBMRecovery.erProb n p (edge weights clamp01 p); reason: clamp01 is the identity on [0, 1], which contains every p of the statement
--   - reading: Theorem 1.1 (informal): 'whp' and 'with probability o(1)' → the bounds 1 − exp(−n^ε) and 1 − exp(−(ε²/2)L log(np)) of Theorems 2.1 and 2.3, for all n ≥ N; reason: Theorem 1.1 is labelled informal and points to Theorems 2.1 and 2.3 as its formal versions
--   - generalization: ε ∈ (0, 1/2) for both halves → every ε > 0, the Greedy clause guarded by ε < 1/2; reason: Theorem 2.3 holds for every ε > 0; at ε ∈ (0, 1/2) both clauses are the source's
--   - encoding: C = C(ε, κ) for each half → one C (and one N) for both clauses; reason: Theorem 1.1 asserts one C; taking the larger of the two constants only shrinks the range of p
--   - generalization: one fixed arbitrary ordering 1, …, n and an arbitrary choice of each u_t → every vertex order σ (a permutation of Fin n) and every choice rule pick; reason: the source's run is one of these runs for any graph, so the statement specializes to it
--   - encoding: choose an arbitrary u_t ∈ N(t) ∩ B_{t−1} → u_t = pick t (N(t) ∩ B_{t−1}) if it is a candidate, else the least candidate; reason: every choice sequence is realized by some pick; the run is total
--   - encoding: Definition 2.2: choices are functions of previous choices and revealed adjacencies → rules next/accept of (G, S, M) with locality fields; an inspected vertex chosen means termination, an illegal partner means no acceptance; reason: every algorithm of Definition 2.2 is such a structure and every such structure runs as one
--   - generalization: auxiliary randomness independent of the graph, every choice a measurable function of the history and the randomness → any probability space (Ω, μ) and A : Ω → OnlineAlg n with every event {ξ : output (A ξ) G = M} measurable; probability Σ_G P[G] · μ{ξ : …}; reason: an algorithm of Definition 2.2 with seed ξ gives such a family (measurable choices make the output measurable); only output measurability is required, so the class is at least the source's
--   - encoding: the seed's probability space, in any universe → Ω : Type (universe 0); reason: a theorem cannot quantify over universes inside its statement; pushing the seed forward to OnlineAlg n (a Type, with the σ-algebra generated by the output events) gives an algorithm in this form with the same probabilities
-- source:
--   Dhawan, Kızıldağ, Maitra, Mao, Wee, Half-Optimal Induced Matchings in Random Graphs: Online and Low-Degree Algorithms, arXiv:2610.40369 (held version, sha256 a1b329a1…), Theorem 1.1, p.4 (PDF p.6); Theorem 2.1, p.9 (PDF p.11); Theorem 2.3, p.10 (PDF p.12)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LPCommunity_SBMRecovery_Setting
import Definitions.Def_HalfIndMatch_Lval
import Definitions.Def_HalfIndMatch_greedyOut
import Definitions.Def_HalfIndMatch_OnlineAlg

open LPCommunity.SBMRecovery MeasureTheory

namespace HalfIndMatch

/-- Theorem 1.1, p.4, in the precise form of Theorems 2.1 and 2.3: for every `κ ∈ (0, 1)` and
`ε > 0` there are `C = C(ε, κ) > 0` and `N` such that for all `n ≥ N` and all `C/n ≤ p ≤ 1 − κ`:
1. if `ε < 1/2`, with probability at least `1 − exp(−n^ε)` Greedy (for every vertex order and every
   choice rule) finds an induced matching with at least `(1/2 − ε)L` edges;
2. every online algorithm of Definition 2.2, with arbitrary auxiliary randomness, ends with at most
   `(1/2 + ε)L` edges with probability at least `1 − exp(−(ε²/2) L log(np))`.

Source: Dhawan, Kızıldağ, Maitra, Mao, Wee, *Half-Optimal Induced Matchings in Random Graphs: Online and Low-Degree Algorithms*, arXiv:2610.40369 (held version, sha256 a1b329a1…), Theorem 1.1, p.4 (PDF p.6); Theorem 2.1, p.9 (PDF p.11); Theorem 2.3, p.10
(PDF p.12).

**Formalization Note** One constant `C` serves both clauses (increasing `C` only shrinks the range of
`p`). `ε > 0` throughout, with the Greedy clause guarded by `ε < 1/2` (Theorem 2.1's range; Theorem
2.3 holds for every `ε > 0`). Randomized algorithms and the joint law are encoded as in
`theorem_2_3`; the Greedy event quantifies over every order and choice rule, as in `theorem_2_1`. -/
theorem theorem_1_1 (κ ε : ℝ) (hκ0 : 0 < κ) (hκ1 : κ < 1) (hε0 : 0 < ε) :
    ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧ ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ n : ℕ, N ≤ n → ∀ p : ℝ, C / n ≤ p → p ≤ 1 - κ →
      (ε < 1 / 2 →
        1 - Real.exp (-((n : ℝ) ^ ε)) ≤
          erProb n p (fun G => ∀ (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) (pick : ℕ → Finset (Fin n) → Fin n),
            (1 / 2 - ε) * Lval n p ≤ ((greedyOut G σ pick).card : ℝ))) ∧
      ∀ (Ω : Type) [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
        (A : Ω → OnlineAlg n),
        (∀ (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (M : Finset (Sym2 (Fin n))),
          MeasurableSet {ξ : Ω | output (A ξ) G = M}) →
        1 - Real.exp (-(ε ^ 2 / 2 * Lval n p * Real.log ((n : ℝ) * p))) ≤
          ∑ G : SimpleGraph (Fin n), edgeWeight G (fun _ _ => p) *
            μ.real {ξ : Ω | ((output (A ξ) G).card : ℝ) ≤ (1 / 2 + ε) * Lval n p} := by sorry

end HalfIndMatch
